 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-aborts-on-error — any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open (2026-09-11), overridden by the maintainer.
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files are
+  still only warned about (exit 0 when nothing fails).
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e`, and swallowing internal errors to force `exit 0`.
+- Reason: the maintainer prefers a check that surfaces its own failures over one that silently passes.
+- Known risk: the original fail-open decision came from a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file blocking every
+  commit (2026-09-10). The `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard skips deleted files; keep it.
